::ENG4_U05_Pre_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which sentence is written in standard English? {
=A. The evidence is credible because it uses recent data.#Correct. The sentence is complete and clear.
~B. The evidence credible because recent data.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
~C. The evidence is credible it uses recent data.#Incorrect. This is a run-on sentence.
~D. Because the evidence credible.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
}
::ENG4_U05_Pre_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.V.02] The phrase status quo means: {
~A. a temporary emergency.#Incorrect. Status quo does not mean emergency.
=B. the existing state of things.#Correct. Status quo means the current condition.
~C. a legal punishment.#Incorrect. This is not the phrase meaning.
~D. a source citation.#Incorrect. Status quo is not a citation.
}
::ENG4_U05_Pre_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: The report called the plan flexible, but critics called it unstable. What is the connotative difference? {
~A. Flexible and unstable both praise the plan.#Incorrect. Unstable is critical.
~B. Flexible means permanent.#Incorrect. Flexible means adaptable.
=C. Flexible suggests adaptability, while unstable suggests lack of reliability.#Correct. This explains the difference in connotation.
~D. Unstable means carefully planned.#Incorrect. It suggests the opposite.
}
::ENG4_U05_Pre_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Which revision best improves the draft, "The article is good because it has stuff"? {
~A. The article is good because it has stuff.#Incorrect. This remains vague.
~B. The article is nice and good and has many things.#Incorrect. This is still vague.
~C. The article is there.#Incorrect. This does not improve the writing.
=D. The article is useful because it provides cost data that helps readers evaluate the proposal.#Correct. This revision improves precision and purpose.
}
::ENG4_U05_Pre_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The schedule is provisional and may change after the board vote. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
=A. The schedule is temporary and could change later.#Correct. This preserves the meaning of provisional.
~B. The schedule is final.#Incorrect. It may change.
~C. The schedule is hidden.#Incorrect. Hidden is not implied.
~D. The schedule has been cancelled.#Incorrect. The passage does not say cancelled.
}
::ENG4_U05_Pre_Q06::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Which sentence uses academic vocabulary precisely? {
~A. The thing is good because it is good.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=B. The author's diction creates a skeptical tone toward the proposal.#Correct. This uses diction and tone precisely.
~C. The author has words and stuff.#Incorrect. This is informal and vague.
~D. The passage is very wordy in some way.#Incorrect. This is imprecise.
}
::ENG4_U05_Pre_Q07::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which revision correctly joins the ideas? Draft: The paragraph has evidence, it lacks explanation. {
~A. The paragraph has evidence, it lacks explanation.#Incorrect. This is a comma splice.
~B. The paragraph has evidence it lacks explanation.#Incorrect. This is a run-on sentence.
=C. The paragraph has evidence, but it lacks explanation.#Correct. But correctly connects contrasting ideas.
~D. Evidence paragraph lacks explanation has.#Incorrect. This is not standard English.
}
::ENG4_U05_Pre_Q08::[MLA.ENG4.V.02] The prefix sub- often means under or below. What does substandard mean? {
~A. above the standard.#Incorrect. Sub- suggests below.
~B. unrelated to standards.#Incorrect. Standard is part of the word.
~C. exactly equal to the standard.#Incorrect. Sub- does not suggest equal.
=D. below an acceptable standard.#Correct. The word parts support this meaning.
}
::ENG4_U05_Pre_Q09::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: The speaker called the policy a shield for consumers. What does shield suggest? {
=A. protection.#Correct. Shield creates a protective connotation.
~B. decoration.#Incorrect. The context does not suggest decoration.
~C. confusion.#Incorrect. Shield does not suggest confusion here.
~D. secrecy.#Incorrect. Shield suggests protection, not secrecy.
}
::ENG4_U05_Pre_Q10::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Which final revision choice is strongest? {
~A. Add difficult words even if meaning changes.#Incorrect. Revision must preserve meaning.
=B. Improve clarity, precision, conventions, and audience fit.#Correct. This captures Unit 5 revision goals.
~C. Make every sentence longer.#Incorrect. Length is not the goal.
~D. Remove all evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence is often necessary.
}
